## 📐 Event & Trace Model

The deterministic‑replay architecture hinges on a clear contract between *events* that mutate state and *traces* that capture the causal chain of those events.  In practice we model the two concepts as a lightweight, immutable Java domain hierarchy that can be serialized to a Kafka topic and replayed by an Axon‑powered event store.  The following sections walk through the full Java OOP model, a resilient Spring‑Boot endpoint that ingests eBPF‑derived logs, three algorithmic utilities for trace manipulation, a minimal ML‑based anomaly detector, and an agentic AI replay orchestrator.

### 1. Java Domain Model (LLD)

```java
// ── Domain Layer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public interface Traceable {
    UUID getTraceId();
    long getTimestamp();
}

public abstract class AbstractEvent implements Traceable {
    private final UUID traceId;
    private final long timestamp;
    private final UUID eventId;

    protected AbstractEvent(UUID traceId) {
        this.traceId = traceId;
        this.timestamp = System.nanoTime();
        this.eventId = UUID.randomUUID();
    }

    @Override public UUID getTraceId() { return traceId; }
    @Override public long getTimestamp() { return timestamp; }
    public UUID getEventId() { return eventId; }
}

public enum EventType { CREATE, UPDATE, DELETE, HEARTBEAT }

public final class DomainEvent extends AbstractEvent {
    private final EventType type;
    private final String payload;   // JSON blob

    public DomainEvent(UUID traceId, EventType type, String payload) {
        super(traceId);
        this.type = type;
        this.payload = payload;
    }

    public EventType getType() { return type; }
    public String getPayload() { return payload; }
}
```

