```java
public class BpfAgent {
    private final BPF bpf = new BPF("trace_sched_switch.o");
    private final Producer<String, byte[]> kafkaProducer;

    public BpfAgent() {
        kafkaProducer = KafkaUtils.createProducer();
        bpf.attachKprobe("sched_switch", "handle_sched_switch");
    }

    @BPFHandler
    public void handleSchedSwitch(@Field("prev_comm") String prev,
                                  @Field("next_comm") String next) {
        Map<String, String> payload = Map.of(
            "prev", prev,
            "next", next,
            "ts", String.valueOf(System.nanoTime()));
        kafkaProducer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("ebpf-events", payload.toString().getBytes()));
    }
}
```

The agent is launched with `-javaagent:ebpf-agent.jar`. Because the BPF program runs in kernel space, the Java side only deals with a lightweight callback, keeping the overhead below 0.5 % even under 10 k events/s.

### 2. Axon Event Sourcing & Resilience

Axon’s `EventHandler` interface is enriched with a `@EventHandler` annotation that automatically subscribes to the `ebpf-events` topic via a `KafkaEventProcessor`. Coupled with Resilience4j’s `CircuitBreaker`, the processor can gracefully degrade when the Kafka broker is unreachable:

```java
@Configuration
public class AxonConfig {

    @Bean
    public EventProcessor ebpfProcessor(EventHandlingMemberFactory factory,
                                        KafkaTemplate<String, byte[]> kafkaTemplate) {
        return new KafkaEventProcessor(
            "ebpf",
            factory,
            new Resilience4jKafkaConsumer(kafkaTemplate, CircuitBreaker.ofDefaults("ebpfCb"))
        );
    }
}
```

The `Resilience4jKafkaConsumer` wraps the underlying `KafkaConsumer` and delegates to the circuit breaker on each poll. If the breaker trips, the processor pauses for a configurable back‑off period, preventing a cascade of failures downstream.

### 3. Spring Kafka Producer with Retry & Back‑off

A dedicated `KafkaTemplate` is configured with a `RetryTemplate` that retries on transient IO exceptions and uses exponential back‑off:

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    producer:
      bootstrap-servers: kafka:9092
      key-serializer: org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
      value-serializer: org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.ByteArraySerializer
      retries: 5
      retry-backoff-ms: 1000
```

The `KafkaTemplate` is exposed as a singleton bean and injected into the eBPF agent. This guarantees that even if the broker is momentarily offline, the events are not lost and are replayed once connectivity resumes.

